From 62bb6292e35a30ecae6ec138fdb16588a45ae17d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HempFiend <17104973+jaidenscool1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 21:20:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] dynarmic: share the A64 code cache between a process's
 cores

Every core had its own JIT and compiled each block it ran: Mario Kart World
compiled 1.55M blocks for 511k distinct ones, and its boot hitches were almost
all compilation. The cores of a process now share core 0's cache, each keeping
its own state:

- per-core values the code used to embed (tpidr pointers, exclusive monitor
  slots) are read from the core's jit state; the callbacks are one object that
  forwards to the core running on the calling thread
- blocks link through 8-byte slots (jmp [rip+slot], mov rcx,[rip+slot]) set
  with atomic stores, so no instruction is rewritten while another core may
  run it
- each core has its own fast dispatch table (a shared one is written while
  other cores read it; without one every indirect branch took the cache lock)
- invalidation keeps the code space (another core may be in it), unlinks and
  bumps an epoch on which every core empties its RSB and dispatch table

Mario Kart World, boot to race: 510k blocks compiled instead of 1.55M, frames
over 100 ms 20-26 instead of 29-40, race unchanged. Switch Sports Resort runs
as before. USCL_SHARED_JIT=0 gives every core its own cache again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
